WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.229 --> 00:00:04.140
Every week this mystery box shows up at my
doorstep.

00:00:04.140 --> 00:00:08.900
It's one of those subscription boxes, except
instead of dog toys or makeup, it's food from

00:00:08.900 --> 00:00:10.039
local farmers.

00:00:10.039 --> 00:00:13.079
And I never know exactly what I'm gonna get.

00:00:13.080 --> 00:00:18.180
Got some salad greens, asparagus, and red
corn..?

00:00:19.820 --> 00:00:22.860
But this wasn't always such an unusual sight.

00:00:22.920 --> 00:00:26.960
If you look through old seed catalogues like
these ones, you'll see hundreds of varieties

00:00:26.970 --> 00:00:33.550
of corn, with names like "Dibbles' Mammoth,"
"Kendel's Early Giant," and my personal favorite,

00:00:33.550 --> 00:00:35.280
"Potter's Excelsior".

00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:38.200
But none of these varieties exist anymore.

00:00:38.200 --> 00:00:43.040
American farmers used to grow hundreds of
varieties of sweet corn, tomatoes, and other

00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:44.150
edible plants.

00:00:44.150 --> 00:00:48.170
Today, just a tiny fraction of those varieties
are still around.

00:00:48.170 --> 00:00:51.700
So what happened to all these plants?

00:00:54.109 --> 00:00:57.550
For most of our time on this planet, humans
have been hunter gatherers.

00:00:57.550 --> 00:00:59.310
We ate what was nearby.

00:00:59.310 --> 00:01:03.920
This was still true when we invented farming
10,000 years ago, by cultivating wild plants

00:01:03.920 --> 00:01:09.080
like Teosinte in Central America and Thorn
Apple in Africa, the Middle East, and Southeast

00:01:09.080 --> 00:01:10.080
Asia.

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:15.850
Over thousands of years, farmers bred these
wild ancestors into foods like corn and eggplant,

00:01:15.850 --> 00:01:17.700
that we would recognize today.

00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:22.110
As humans moved around the world, so did the
seeds and farmers continued to breed different

00:01:22.110 --> 00:01:25.220
varieties to adapt them to their new environments.

00:01:25.220 --> 00:01:27.660
Which led to a ton of genetic diversity.

00:01:27.660 --> 00:01:31.300
Farmers could raise different genetic varieties
of different crops.

00:01:31.470 --> 00:01:35.700
If disease or pests killed one type, there
were others to fall back on.

00:01:35.700 --> 00:01:40.490
But gradually industrialization and cheap
fossil fuels made us less dependent on what

00:01:40.490 --> 00:01:41.580
grew well nearby.

00:01:41.580 --> 00:01:48.070
"Food on the move, from distant parts of the
world comes the great variety of foods Americans

00:01:48.070 --> 00:01:49.060
demand."

00:01:49.060 --> 00:01:53.940
Most farmers switched from growing a variety
of edible plants to a single crop that was

00:01:53.950 --> 00:01:55.930
easy to process and ship.

00:01:55.930 --> 00:02:00.960
As this model spread beyond the United States,
older varieties of plants and animals disappeared

00:02:00.960 --> 00:02:02.590
from farms around the world.

00:02:02.590 --> 00:02:08.890
By 1970, 90% of the wheat varieties that had
once been grown in China were gone.

00:02:08.890 --> 00:02:13.629
As were 80% of the varieties of maize or corn
that were once grown in Mexico.

00:02:13.629 --> 00:02:22.870
By the summer of 1971, more than 85% of the
corn planted in the US was genetically identical.

00:02:22.870 --> 00:02:27.140
Crop scientists had bred this new corn so
that it grew without a tassel, making it easier

00:02:27.140 --> 00:02:28.719
to harvest.

00:02:28.719 --> 00:02:33.270
But because these plants were genetic copies
of one another, that also made them susceptible

00:02:33.270 --> 00:02:36.900
to the same deadly fungus, Southern Leaf Corn
Blight.

00:02:36.900 --> 00:02:41.840
It took over the US corn crop, costing farmers
and taxpayers millions of dollars.

00:02:41.840 --> 00:02:47.329
And the damage would have continued, if it
weren't for a humble little plant called Teosinte.

00:02:47.329 --> 00:02:53.519
The wild grass native to Oaxaca, Mexico, and
the common ancestor of the 22,000 known varieties

00:02:53.519 --> 00:02:54.650
of corn.

00:02:54.650 --> 00:02:59.659
Teosinte includes a gene for resistance to
the same fungus that was devastating the US

00:02:59.660 --> 00:03:00.920
corn crop.

00:03:00.920 --> 00:03:06.120
Scientists halted the damage by crossbreeding
Teosinte with American corn, but that didn't

00:03:06.129 --> 00:03:09.019
totally solve the problem of genetic diversity.

00:03:09.019 --> 00:03:14.829
Today, more than 40% of the corn grown in
the US is derived from just six inbred lines.

00:03:14.829 --> 00:03:20.489
And seed companies, driven by profit, can
repackage genetic copies of the same seeds

00:03:20.489 --> 00:03:22.150
for different prices.

00:03:22.150 --> 00:03:25.689
Farmers plant them, thinking that they're
genetically diversifying their fields when

00:03:25.689 --> 00:03:27.299
really they're not.

00:03:27.299 --> 00:03:33.549
Since the corn crisis in 1971 disease has
ravaged genetically uniform crops of beans,

00:03:33.549 --> 00:03:36.460
rice tomatoes, and bananas.

00:03:36.460 --> 00:03:39.129
And it's about to get worse.

00:03:39.129 --> 00:03:43.011
The plants we eat have spent thousands of
years evolving to grow in specific conditions,

00:03:43.011 --> 00:03:49.900
conditions we are changing rapidly by releasing
more and more carbon dioxide into the atmosphere.

00:03:49.900 --> 00:03:55.170
We depend on corn, wheat, and rice for more
than 60% of our global calories.

00:03:55.480 --> 00:04:00.099
And by 2050, we'll have 2 billion more people
to feed.

00:04:00.099 --> 00:04:04.879
But because of climate change, we'll actually
be producing less of all three of these crops.

00:04:04.879 --> 00:04:09.840
We're going to need plants that can grow in
radically different conditions and the more

00:04:09.840 --> 00:04:13.639
genetic varieties we save, the better protected
we'll be.

00:04:13.639 --> 00:04:18.040
There are seed banks all over the world where
scientists, indigenous communities, and farmers

00:04:18.040 --> 00:04:20.990
are all preserving older seed varieties.

00:04:20.990 --> 00:04:25.840
But thousands have already been lost, which
is why it's so critical to preserve the genetic

00:04:25.840 --> 00:04:27.340
diversity we still have.

00:04:27.340 --> 00:04:29.930
The weird stuff, like red popcorn.

00:04:29.930 --> 00:04:35.840
And the best way to save the seeds that might
save us one day, is to grow them and eat them.

